      "id": "word-perdona--apology",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "perdona",
      "answerEn": "sorry; excuse me (informal)",
      "noteEn": "Perdona is the familiar singular command of perdonar, “to forgive.” It is commonly used to apologize or to get one person's attention.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Perdona, ¿tienes un minuto?",
        "en": "Excuse me, do you have a minute?"
      },
      "tags": ["word", "apologies", "commands", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-yo--self-reference",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "yo",
      "answerEn": "me; myself (self-reference rather than a sentence subject)",
      "noteEn": "Yo normally means “I” when it is the subject of a verb. When someone identifies, labels, or addresses their own self, natural English may use “me” or “myself,” while Spanish still uses yo.",
      "example": {
        "es": "La persona de la foto soy yo.",
        "en": "The person in the photo is me."
      },
      "tags": ["word", "pronouns", "self-reference", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-tener-miedo",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "tener miedo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone is afraid?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of tener followed by miedo.",
      "noteEn": "Spanish treats fear as something a person has. Change tener to match the person and time, but keep miedo unchanged: tengo miedo, tiene miedo, or tenían miedo.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Luis tiene miedo.",
        "en": "Luis is afraid."
      },
      "tags": ["phrase", "feelings", "tener", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-quizas-subjunctive-uncertainty",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "quizás seguido de subjuntivo",
      "questionEn": "How can you present an idea as an uncertain possibility with quizás?",
      "answerEn": "Put quizás before the idea; when you treat it as uncertain, use a special possibility form of the verb.",
      "noteEn": "That possibility form is called the subjunctive. It marks the idea as possible rather than asserting it as a fact: viene can change to venga, and es can change to sea. Quizás can also occur with an ordinary statement form when the speaker presents the possibility more neutrally or confidently.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Quizás Marta venga mañana.",
        "en": "Maybe Marta will come tomorrow."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "possibility", "subjunctive", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-lo-predicate-proform",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "lo con ser o parecer",
      "questionEn": "How do you avoid repeating a description or classification after ser or parecer?",
      "answerEn": "Put the unchanged word lo before a form of ser or parecer to stand for that description or classification.",
      "noteEn": "This lo does not replace a particular masculine noun and does not change for a feminine or plural subject. It stands for the whole property, identity, or classification already understood, as in lo es or lo parece.",
      "example": {
        "es": "¿Es difícil? No lo parece.",
        "en": "Is it difficult? It doesn't seem so."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "pronouns", "descriptions", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-puede-que-subjunctive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "puede que seguido de subjuntivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that something may or might be true or happen?",
      "answerEn": "Use puede que, then put the following verb in a special possibility form.",
      "noteEn": "The special form is called the subjunctive: it presents the following idea as possible rather than as a plain fact. Puede que stays fixed, while the next verb changes to match its own subject; for example, llega becomes llegue and es becomes sea.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Puede que Ana llegue tarde.",
        "en": "Ana may arrive late."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "possibility", "subjunctive", "A2"]
    }
  ],
